preConditionHook:
// THE FIRST BAR OF THE SESSION WAS A DOJI -- LATCHED for the rest of the day
// as a trading-range-day bias, favouring fades over breakout continuation.
//
// THE LATCH IS THE POINT, and the only reason this is a rule rather than
// DojiBar ANDed with a first-bar filter: the doji fires once, at the open, and
// biases the WHOLE day. Read one bar at a time it would gate one bar a session
// and nothing else. The verdict is recomputed only on a session boundary and
// otherwise held -- rangeDayBias is a class member, so it persists across bars
// by construction, exactly as the EL variable does.
//
// SESSION MODEL, per the standing decision: day_of(ctx.Time(0)) >
// day_of(ctx.Time(1)), the EasyLanguage twin's date > date[1]. The comparison
// is on integer day serials, not prices, so it is exact and does not go
// through el_gt.
//
// The doji test is DojiBar's, unchanged: std::fabs(close - open) against the
// bar's OWN range, EL's AbsValue. Book values 0.1 (R21-15) and 0.25 (VP3-04);
// default 0.25.
//
// NOT FirstBarDojiInsidePriorClosingRange, which adds a size test and a
// containment test in yesterday's closing range and does NOT latch; the two
// fire on different days and for different spans.
//
// WARM-UP: rangeDayBias starts false like every EL variable, so the rule is
// false until the first session boundary the study sees -- a first calculated
// bar falling mid-session gates nothing until the next open, which is the
// conservative reading and is what EL does.
//
// Direction-agnostic -- it is a day-type bias, not a direction -- so both sides
// are identical text. Intraday bars only.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    rangeDayBias = el_le(std::fabs(close[0] - open[0]), dojiFraction * (high[0] - low[0]));
}
